=== Does aggregation make the test more or less sensitive? ===
  Parkinson      probe-level critical 0.0388 -> region-level critical 0.0614 (+58%)
  Schizophrenia  probe-level critical 0.0353 -> region-level critical 0.0569 (+61%)
  Alzheimer      probe-level critical 0.0163 -> region-level critical 0.0286 (+75%)
  Depression     probe-level critical 0.0274 -> region-level critical 0.0625 (+128%)
  IBD            probe-level critical 0.0413 -> region-level critical 0.0711 (+72%)
  Critical values generally rise, so aggregation is on the whole less sensitive in these data: noise is averaged out, 
  but the loss of independent units weighs more. The region level can therefore confirm but not refute -
  a disorder that is not significant at region level but significant at probe level must be treated as 'this test is insensitive for it'.

=== Interpretation for the two negative results ===
  Alzheimer      probe +0.0024 -> region +0.0002; region-level critical 0.0286, still not significant after aggregation
  Depression     probe -0.0028 -> region -0.0307; region-level critical 0.0625, still not significant after aggregation
  Both negatives hold at region level; single-probe noise is not their cause.
  After aggregation Alzheimer's disease changes from +0.0024 to +0.0002, depression from -0.0028 to -0.0307 - neither moves in the positive direction.

